  "live_records": {
    "q-124446": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124446",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "What is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?",
      "worked_solution": "To express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${245}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14248083/assets/312fc3581d9da413254a0eb4dbc64091b286675492821838ba9a65ee06f45a7c.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={245}^{∘}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={245}^{∘}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={65}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{65}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{65}^{∘}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
