    "q-124455": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124455",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "What is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?",
      "worked_solution": "To express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${140}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14248083/assets/5f2b641a3a765f65f1d7d11837193341edb6876dfaea45e9eeaf1d7610178435.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={140}^{∘}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={180}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{140}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
