    "q-156075": {
      "dom_id": "step-q156075",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "What is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?",
      "worked_solution": "To express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${197}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14248083/assets/d483d80833650a101f993d9f66ffb9c786681240a4bfa4e255fb116cc7ad5fd2.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={197}^{∘}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={197}^{∘}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={17}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{17}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{17}^{∘}.\n$$",
