    "q-156605": {
      "dom_id": "step-q156605",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?",
      "worked_solution": "To express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${310}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14248083/assets/70325d2a1d2d252e20eea4f4940b8dc03b21c737709ffe65d95f36b51bb63fcf.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={310}^{∘}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={360}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{310}^{∘} \\\\  & ={50}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{50}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the tangent ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{50}^{∘}.\n$$",
